WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.367 --> 00:00:02.701
-Our first guest tonight
is a Grammy-nominated,

00:00:02.701 --> 00:00:04.767
Emmy-winning writer,
comedian, and actress.

00:00:04.767 --> 00:00:06.334
You can see her
next in "Maestro,"

00:00:06.334 --> 00:00:08.434
which is
in select theaters now

00:00:08.434 --> 00:00:10.167
and streams
on Netflix December 20th.

00:00:10.167 --> 00:00:11.167
Let's take a look.

00:00:11.167 --> 00:00:12.701
-Have you met the gang?

00:00:12.701 --> 00:00:14.534
Betty and Adolph.
They're just a riot.

00:00:14.534 --> 00:00:15.767
-Hello.
-Hello.

00:00:15.767 --> 00:00:17.534
-Ellen. I'm sure
you've run into her

00:00:17.534 --> 00:00:19.167
in mother's studio, no doubt.

00:00:19.167 --> 00:00:21.501
-Hello, Felicia.
-Who did I miss?

00:00:21.501 --> 00:00:23.133
Have I missed anyone?

00:00:23.133 --> 00:00:24.801
-Just the piano player.
-[ Laughs ]

00:00:24.801 --> 00:00:27.567
-Well, I figured you needed
no introduction.

00:00:27.567 --> 00:00:29.334
-Hello.
I'm Lenny.

00:00:29.334 --> 00:00:30.701
-Please welcome back to the show

00:00:30.701 --> 00:00:32.901
our friend Sarah Silverman,
everybody!

00:00:32.901 --> 00:00:34.901
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:34.901 --> 00:00:44.901
♪♪

00:00:44.901 --> 00:00:49.467
♪♪

00:00:49.467 --> 00:00:51.200
Welcome back.

00:00:51.200 --> 00:00:53.033
-This is so nice of you
to come out east.

00:00:53.033 --> 00:00:55.000
You're from your home in L.A.
right before the holidays.

00:00:55.000 --> 00:00:56.567
-Yeah, yeah.
Oh, God, yeah.

00:00:56.567 --> 00:01:01.167
I just spent a week with my,
um, common-law in-laws.

00:01:01.167 --> 00:01:02.567
-Okay.
So this is Rory.

00:01:02.567 --> 00:01:04.000
How long have you
and Rory been dating now

00:01:04.000 --> 00:01:05.400
to have a common-law in-law?

00:01:05.400 --> 00:01:07.000
-Well, I think it's supposed
to be 10 years,

00:01:07.000 --> 00:01:08.534
but we've been together
three years.

00:01:08.534 --> 00:01:09.801
-Okay, gotcha.
[ Laughter ]

00:01:09.801 --> 00:01:10.901
That's enough.
So, common-law in-laws.

00:01:10.901 --> 00:01:12.133
Did you luck into it?

00:01:12.133 --> 00:01:13.667
Do you have a good pair
of common-law in-laws?

00:01:13.667 --> 00:01:16.033
-Oh, they're so great.
I love them so much.

00:01:16.033 --> 00:01:18.067
And my, uh -- his dad,

00:01:18.067 --> 00:01:21.667
Big Jim, um,
is a real raconteur.

00:01:21.667 --> 00:01:23.334
-Okay, tells a story.
-So it's fun, yeah.

00:01:23.334 --> 00:01:24.534
Oh, he tells such stories.

00:01:24.534 --> 00:01:27.868
They're not --
They're not original stories.

00:01:27.868 --> 00:01:29.868
They're not -- you know,
he didn't make them up,

00:01:29.868 --> 00:01:32.501
but he just -- like --
Wow.

00:01:32.501 --> 00:01:34.501
I sat with him
right before I left,

00:01:34.501 --> 00:01:39.200
and he told me the entire...

00:01:39.200 --> 00:01:44.100
scene-for-scene story of
the movie "Legends of the Fall."

00:01:44.100 --> 00:01:45.434
-Oh, wow.
-Yeah.

00:01:45.434 --> 00:01:47.167
I had never seen it.
-Yeah.

00:01:47.167 --> 00:01:48.767
-It's good!
-It is good.

00:01:48.767 --> 00:01:51.267
-A little long.
-It's a -- It is a long movie.

00:01:51.267 --> 00:01:52.868
I would imagine,
without the visuals,

00:01:52.868 --> 00:01:54.734
it might be a little longer.
-Yeah, well...

00:01:54.734 --> 00:01:56.634
Theater of the mind,
like the Mummy Christmas.

00:01:56.634 --> 00:01:58.000
-Yeah, like,
Fred's always talking about

00:01:58.000 --> 00:01:59.734
theater of the mind.
-I like theater of the mind.

00:01:59.734 --> 00:02:02.467
-Um, this is --
speaking of visual,

00:02:02.467 --> 00:02:05.100
this is a beautiful film
that Bradley Cooper directed.

00:02:05.100 --> 00:02:06.501
-Isn't it?
-It's gorgeous.

00:02:06.501 --> 00:02:07.801
-A feast for the senses.

00:02:07.801 --> 00:02:09.667
-It is a feast for the senses.

00:02:09.667 --> 00:02:12.834
This is his film, "Maestro,"
about Leonard Bernstein.

00:02:12.834 --> 00:02:14.501
You play his sister.
-Uh-huh.

00:02:14.501 --> 00:02:16.701
-And you're wonderful in it.
-Thank you.

00:02:16.701 --> 00:02:18.267
Oh, thank you.
-Did you love you --

00:02:18.267 --> 00:02:20.067
-Did you love...
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:02:20.067 --> 00:02:21.767
...period costumes?

00:02:21.767 --> 00:02:23.601
-Um. Oh, yeah.
It's really --

00:02:23.601 --> 00:02:25.200
It does all the work for you.

00:02:25.200 --> 00:02:28.534
You got a costume, and they make
your hair old-timey,

00:02:28.534 --> 00:02:31.601
and they put stuff on you,
and you get a cigarette,

00:02:31.601 --> 00:02:33.701
and then --
And the dialect coach

00:02:33.701 --> 00:02:35.834
tells you how to talk, and then
you just say the words.

00:02:35.834 --> 00:02:37.334
-And then it looks great.
It looks great.

00:02:37.334 --> 00:02:39.400
-Yeah.
-How well did you know --

00:02:39.400 --> 00:02:42.234
Did you know Bradley
before this?

00:02:42.234 --> 00:02:46.033
-Yes. You know, it's --
That's such a trick --

00:02:46.033 --> 00:02:50.100
I did an interview with,
like, a newspaper, uh, lady.

00:02:50.100 --> 00:02:52.100
You know,
"Hey, what's the scoop?"

00:02:52.100 --> 00:02:53.901
No.
-Yeah, we all know

00:02:53.901 --> 00:02:57.300
how newspaper ladies talk.
[ Laughter ]

00:02:57.300 --> 00:02:59.000
-Person.
-Yeah.

00:02:59.000 --> 00:03:00.834
-Newspaper person.
-That's what it's called.

00:03:00.834 --> 00:03:03.734
-Yeah.
-Um, and, uh...

00:03:03.734 --> 00:03:05.400
she's asking me about "Maestro,"

00:03:05.400 --> 00:03:07.934
and she said, "Did you --
How well do you know Bradley?"

00:03:07.934 --> 00:03:09.334
and I said, uh --

00:03:09.334 --> 00:03:10.467
You know,
I didn't want to be like,

00:03:10.467 --> 00:03:12.434
"Oh, we're buddies."
You know, I don't --

00:03:12.434 --> 00:03:14.334
You know how it's not --

00:03:14.334 --> 00:03:18.234
You know, I go,
"Uh, I know him.

00:03:18.234 --> 00:03:22.000
I've known him peripherally
for a long time, you know?

00:03:22.000 --> 00:03:23.400
And we're not buddies.

00:03:23.400 --> 00:03:26.534
But, you know, we've known
each other a bit here and there,

00:03:26.534 --> 00:03:28.167
but, you know, not --"

00:03:28.167 --> 00:03:30.367
And then I did, like --

00:03:30.367 --> 00:03:32.901
We've been doing these
Q&amp;A's after screenings,

00:03:32.901 --> 00:03:36.033
and I was backstage
like a week later

00:03:36.033 --> 00:03:39.501
with Bradley and Carrie and

00:03:39.501 --> 00:03:44.334
and Matt Bomer,
and Bradley goes,

00:03:44.334 --> 00:03:48.734
"You know, an interviewer
for the piece on you called me.

00:03:48.734 --> 00:03:50.801
She said that you said
we didn't know each other well.

00:03:50.801 --> 00:03:53.567
I was like,
'We're totally good friends.'"

00:03:53.567 --> 00:03:55.300
And I was like,
"That's so weird.

00:03:55.300 --> 00:03:57.200
Yeah, we're totally
good friends."

00:03:57.200 --> 00:03:59.300
And I'm just telling --
Now if he sees this,

00:03:59.300 --> 00:04:01.133
I'll be like, "I don't know.
I didn't want to be like,

00:04:01.133 --> 00:04:03.434
'Yeah, we're good friends.'"
I mean, I don't know.

00:04:03.434 --> 00:04:06.467
I don't have his e-mail address.
But I have a cellphone number.

00:04:06.467 --> 00:04:08.200
-Yeah.
[ Laughter ]

00:04:08.200 --> 00:04:09.734
I feel like
that's a better friend.

00:04:09.734 --> 00:04:11.434
Like, if we --
Like, for example,

00:04:11.434 --> 00:04:13.067
if you only
had someone's e-mail,

00:04:13.067 --> 00:04:16.267
I would say that person has told
you, "We're not friends."

00:04:16.267 --> 00:04:17.601
-Right.
-But I've only had

00:04:17.601 --> 00:04:19.968
his cellphone number
since the movie.

00:04:19.968 --> 00:04:21.734
-Okay.
-Making the movie.

00:04:21.734 --> 00:04:24.133
But we -- you know,
I've seen him around.

00:04:24.133 --> 00:04:26.133
-Was the newspaper person
ever, like,

00:04:26.133 --> 00:04:27.534
"Just give me
a straight answer, kid!"

00:04:27.534 --> 00:04:29.400
-Eh!
-"Do you know him or not?!"

00:04:29.400 --> 00:04:30.667
[ Laughter ]

00:04:30.667 --> 00:04:32.834
What do you mean 'peripherally
for a long time'?"

00:04:32.834 --> 00:04:35.033
-What is it to know someone?
-Oh, my God.

00:04:35.033 --> 00:04:36.901
-"Look, I'm trying --
I'm on deadline."

00:04:36.901 --> 00:04:38.534
[ Laughter ]

00:04:38.534 --> 00:04:39.868
I want to congratulate you,
as well.

00:04:39.868 --> 00:04:41.000
-You have, like, the "press"
thing in your hat.

00:04:41.000 --> 00:04:42.334
-Of course.
That's how you know, yeah.

00:04:42.334 --> 00:04:43.634
That's the only way you know.
Otherwise, that's when

00:04:43.634 --> 00:04:44.701
like,
"Can I see your credentials?"

00:04:44.701 --> 00:04:46.067
and they show you their hat.
-Like...

00:04:46.067 --> 00:04:47.334
-Yeah.
[ Laughter ]

00:04:47.334 --> 00:04:50.400
You had a wonderful special,

00:04:50.400 --> 00:04:53.968
and it was nominated for both
a Grammy and a Golden Globe.

00:04:53.968 --> 00:05:01.701
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:05:01.701 --> 00:05:03.801
"Someone You Love"
is wonderful.

00:05:03.801 --> 00:05:05.234
-That's what it's called --
"Someone You love."

00:05:05.234 --> 00:05:07.667
-Yes. And this is, uh --

00:05:07.667 --> 00:05:08.868
You can see it on Max.
Correct?

00:05:08.868 --> 00:05:11.100
-That's right.
-And you filmed it --

00:05:11.100 --> 00:05:12.367
You've done --
This is your fourth special.

00:05:12.367 --> 00:05:13.534
You've done your first three
in L.A.,

00:05:13.534 --> 00:05:14.767
which has been home
for a long time.

00:05:14.767 --> 00:05:16.267
But you went back
to the Wilbur Theatre,

00:05:16.267 --> 00:05:17.901
wonderful theater in Boston.
-Yes!

00:05:17.901 --> 00:05:18.968
-And we're New Englanders.

00:05:18.968 --> 00:05:20.667
Did you feel like
it was nice to do --

00:05:20.667 --> 00:05:22.501
I mean, did they feel like
a hometown crowd still to you?

00:05:22.501 --> 00:05:26.400
-"Hometown hero makes good!"
-Yeah.

00:05:26.400 --> 00:05:27.834
"Barely knows Bradley Cooper."

00:05:27.834 --> 00:05:29.968
[ Laughter ]

00:05:29.968 --> 00:05:33.534
-I love playing the Wilbur.
It's always good there.

00:05:33.534 --> 00:05:37.801
And it's Boston, which feels
like the closest to home...

00:05:37.801 --> 00:05:39.000
-Yes, of course.

00:05:39.000 --> 00:05:42.868
-...without actually going home.
-Right.

00:05:42.868 --> 00:05:45.267
It's a good like hour buffer
from where we grew up.

00:05:45.267 --> 00:05:47.567
-It's a 40-minute,
45-minute "buffah."

00:05:47.567 --> 00:05:49.767
-Yeah. It's a good to have
a little "buffah."

00:05:49.767 --> 00:05:51.834
[ Laughter ]
Speaking of Boston,

00:05:51.834 --> 00:05:54.734
you, obviously, have been
nominated for some awards here,

00:05:54.734 --> 00:05:56.200
obviously for this.

00:05:56.200 --> 00:06:00.567
You won an Emmy
for music and lyrics.

00:06:00.567 --> 00:06:02.767
I didn't realize
that your Emmy is for

00:06:02.767 --> 00:06:04.334
"I'm [bleep] Matt Damon."
There you are.

00:06:04.334 --> 00:06:05.734
[ Laughter ]

00:06:05.734 --> 00:06:07.968
I mean, this is
a Boston collaboration.

00:06:07.968 --> 00:06:10.000
This is my --
This is my "Good Will Hunting,"

00:06:10.000 --> 00:06:12.000
this song.
[ Laughter ]

00:06:12.000 --> 00:06:13.300
Was that -- I mean,
that must have been so --

00:06:13.300 --> 00:06:14.534
I don't know.
It's a ways back now.

00:06:14.534 --> 00:06:16.000
But it must have been
so fun to do.

00:06:16.000 --> 00:06:17.901
And the response to it --
I remember this was, like,

00:06:17.901 --> 00:06:19.467
the first thing
that everybody was like --

00:06:19.467 --> 00:06:20.968
that I knew that everybody's
like, "You've got to watch that

00:06:20.968 --> 00:06:22.667
on YouTube."
-Yeah, like, went "viral,"

00:06:22.667 --> 00:06:23.901
you know, back in the day.

00:06:23.901 --> 00:06:25.567
And I remember, after that,
people would like,

00:06:25.567 --> 00:06:27.100
want me to make videos for them.

00:06:27.100 --> 00:06:29.434
And they're like, "We'd like you
to make a viral video."

00:06:29.434 --> 00:06:31.601
And I was like, "Yeah,
everyone would like to make

00:06:31.601 --> 00:06:33.868
a viral video.
That's not your decision.

00:06:33.868 --> 00:06:35.267
Like, you have
no control over it."

00:06:35.267 --> 00:06:36.901
-I was like, "You know,
it's just a new word

00:06:36.901 --> 00:06:39.367
for 'popular,'"
like, when they used "viral."

00:06:39.367 --> 00:06:41.534
-"Could you make a smash hit?"
-Yeah.

00:06:41.534 --> 00:06:43.367
-"Sure, I'd love to."
-"I would love to.

00:06:43.367 --> 00:06:45.067
They just --
They don't always work."

00:06:45.067 --> 00:06:48.000
You've had a career
full of smash hits,

00:06:48.000 --> 00:06:49.868
but I did not know...
-What are these pictures?

00:06:49.868 --> 00:06:52.334
This is one
of your first --

00:06:52.334 --> 00:06:54.000
Which "Star Trek" franchise --

00:06:54.000 --> 00:06:56.834
-I'm here promoting
"I'm [bleep] Matt Damon"

00:06:56.834 --> 00:06:58.767
from 2007...
-Yeah, and then...

00:06:58.767 --> 00:07:00.767
-...and, uh,
"Star Trek: Voyager" from 1997.

00:07:00.767 --> 00:07:03.968
-But all all of these things
are later than, you know,

00:07:03.968 --> 00:07:06.667
when Leonard Bernstein was born.
So these are all more recent.

00:07:06.667 --> 00:07:08.434
-Okay, good.
-But "Maestro" --

00:07:08.434 --> 00:07:10.367
do see "Maestro," but I also
want to talk about this.

00:07:10.367 --> 00:07:11.567
So, "I'm [bleep] Matt Damon" --

00:07:11.567 --> 00:07:13.767
Basically,
what you're saying is,

00:07:13.767 --> 00:07:15.968
Bernstein, Silverman,
basically the same.

00:07:15.968 --> 00:07:18.267
-I'm saying you're
a composer, as well, yeah.

00:07:18.267 --> 00:07:22.300
All right. But here you go.
This is "Star Trek: Voyager"?

00:07:22.300 --> 00:07:24.901
-Oh, first of all, I kiss him.

00:07:24.901 --> 00:07:26.267
I love that you have
this picture.

00:07:26.267 --> 00:07:27.467
I don't have my glasses on,

00:07:27.467 --> 00:07:29.601
but this does look like
the back of my ass.

00:07:29.601 --> 00:07:31.601
-Yeah.
-I kissed him

00:07:31.601 --> 00:07:33.133
at the end of the episode,

00:07:33.133 --> 00:07:35.267
and he was standing
on an apple box

00:07:35.267 --> 00:07:38.267
because God forbid you're
the same height as the girl.

00:07:38.267 --> 00:07:40.501
-Yeah.
-And then I made out with him

00:07:40.501 --> 00:07:41.901
in real life.
-Oh, wow!

00:07:41.901 --> 00:07:43.734
-Yeah.
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:07:43.734 --> 00:07:45.000
-Can you tell us what job
you had

00:07:45.000 --> 00:07:47.033
on this episode
of "Star Trek: Voyager"?

00:07:47.033 --> 00:07:48.834
-I play a scientist.

00:07:48.834 --> 00:07:49.968
[ Laughter ]

00:07:49.968 --> 00:07:51.434
And I went for the fitting,
and I go --

00:07:51.434 --> 00:07:53.100
They've got me in, like,
a half shirt.

00:07:53.100 --> 00:07:54.834
-Yeah.
-"Yeah, you're a scientist with,

00:07:54.834 --> 00:07:56.901
like, a push-up bra
and a half shirt."

00:07:56.901 --> 00:07:59.501
-Yeah. And it looks like
a very tiny backpack.

00:07:59.501 --> 00:08:01.501
Like, it feels like a scientist
would have a bigger backpack.

00:08:01.501 --> 00:08:04.234
-Right.
And then that's my VW van,

00:08:04.234 --> 00:08:05.868
which for a very long time,

00:08:05.868 --> 00:08:08.467
I played someone
who drove a VW van.

00:08:08.467 --> 00:08:10.734
I think three different things
I had this car.

00:08:10.734 --> 00:08:12.701
-Really? You probably
got really good at it.

00:08:12.701 --> 00:08:13.767
At that point,
they were like --

00:08:13.767 --> 00:08:15.267
Your agent was like,
"She can do it."

00:08:15.267 --> 00:08:18.200
-The stick is, like, a long
stick with a ball at the end.

00:08:18.200 --> 00:08:20.601
-It's not easy.
Not any any actor can do it,

00:08:20.601 --> 00:08:21.701
but Sarah can.
-That's right.

00:08:21.701 --> 00:08:23.167
-You're so wonderful
in this movie.

00:08:23.167 --> 00:08:24.901
It's really beautiful, and it's
always so great to see you.

00:08:24.901 --> 00:08:25.934
You guys,
that's Sarah Silverman.

00:08:25.934 --> 00:08:28.267
-I love you.
-"Maestro" is in

00:08:28.267 --> 00:08:30.734
select theaters now and streams
on Netflix December 20th.

00:08:30.734 --> 00:08:32.634
We'll be right back
with Greta Lee.

